[ CUBE: THE CUBE OF FJCC68 FIG. 7, 12 EDGES AND 4 MOVES.  SEEN IN
[ ORTHOGONAL PROJECTION, IT FLIPS FROM ONE ORIENTATION TO THE OTHER AS
[ IT TURNS: NOTHING TELLS ITS FRONT FROM ITS BACK.
[ SHOW IT WITH:   DSPLY (V.CUBE, 2)!   CLOCK (DIALS+1, DI.EX)!
[ DIALS 0, 1 AND 2 THEN TURN IT ABOUT Z, X AND Y.
CUBE:   TEXT /CUBE/
        MOVE (-.99, -.99, -.99) [ THE BACK FACE, THEN THE FRONT
        DRAW (-.99, .99, -.99)
        DRAW (-.99, .99, .99)
        DRAW (-.99, -.99, .99)
        DRAW (-.99, -.99, -.99)
        DRAW (.99, -.99, -.99)
        DRAW (.99, .99, -.99)
        DRAW (.99, .99, .99)
        DRAW (.99, -.99, .99)
        DRAW (.99, -.99, -.99)
        MOVE (-.99, .99, -.99)  [ THE THREE EDGES LEFT, BACK TO FRONT
        DRAW (.99, .99, -.99)
        MOVE (-.99, .99, .99)
        DRAW (.99, .99, .99)
        MOVE (-.99, -.99, .99)
        DRAW (.99, -.99, .99)
        RET
[ THE VIEWER: THE ANGLES ARE READ EACH FRAME FROM THE WORDS WHERE DIALS,
[ A CLOCK ROUTINE, PUTS THEM (AN IMMEDIATE REFERENCE LIST, DSPLY P. 14).
V.CUBE: TEXT /V.CUBE/
        RXYZ'IRL (DIALZ'DIR, DIALX'DIR, DIALY'DIR)
        IMG (CUBE)
        RET
